WEBVTT

00:00.266 --> 00:02.532
- [Instructor] We know that
for x in the open interval

00:02.532 --> 00:04.561
from negative 1/2 to 1/2,

00:04.561 --> 00:06.449
that negative two over one minus two x

00:06.449 --> 00:08.630
is equal to this series.

00:08.630 --> 00:11.374
And it says using this
fact, find the function

00:11.374 --> 00:14.261
that corresponds to the following series.

00:14.261 --> 00:15.471
And like always, pause this video

00:15.471 --> 00:17.514
and see if you can work through it.

00:17.514 --> 00:19.532
All right, so the first
thing you might say is well

00:19.532 --> 00:23.288
how do we know that this
expression is equal to this series?

00:23.288 --> 00:26.504
And you might recognize this
series as a geometric series

00:26.504 --> 00:28.945
where the first term is negative two

00:28.945 --> 00:31.867
and then the common ratio
to get each successive term,

00:31.867 --> 00:36.034
we're multiplying by two x,
we're multiplying by two x.

00:37.771 --> 00:39.316
And so for a geometric series like this,

00:39.316 --> 00:41.506
the sum is gonna be the first term,

00:41.506 --> 00:44.716
negative two over one
minus the common ratio,

00:44.716 --> 00:46.884
which is exactly what we have over there.

00:46.884 --> 00:48.269
So that's why we know that.

00:48.269 --> 00:49.252
And this right over here,

00:49.252 --> 00:51.172
this gives us our radius of convergence,

00:51.172 --> 00:55.788
the x values for which this
thing will actually converge.

00:55.788 --> 00:57.716
But now that we feel good
about this first statement,

00:57.716 --> 01:00.709
let's try to answer their actual question.

01:00.709 --> 01:02.753
So we wanna find the function

01:02.753 --> 01:05.001
that corresponds to the following series.

01:05.001 --> 01:08.858
So my instinct is to say, well
how does this series relate

01:08.858 --> 01:10.645
to the series they gave us?

01:10.645 --> 01:12.921
Let's see, this first
term is a negative two,

01:12.921 --> 01:15.402
this first term is a negative two x,

01:15.402 --> 01:18.562
this is a negative four x, this
is a negative two x squared.

01:18.562 --> 01:22.343
So the thing that might
jump out at you is that

01:22.343 --> 01:27.220
this second series they gave
us is the antiderivative

01:27.220 --> 01:28.066
of this first one.

01:28.066 --> 01:30.077
Or we could say this first
series is a derivative

01:30.077 --> 01:31.015
of the second one.

01:31.015 --> 01:33.645
What's the derivative of
negative two x with respect to x?

01:33.645 --> 01:35.061
Well, it's negative two.

01:35.061 --> 01:36.983
What's the derivative of
negative two x squared

01:36.983 --> 01:38.310
with respect to x?

01:38.310 --> 01:41.941
Well, it's negative four
x and so on and so forth.

01:41.941 --> 01:46.072
And so if we were to
call this, let me call,

01:46.072 --> 01:48.597
let me call this thing right over here,

01:48.597 --> 01:53.439
actually, let me just say
that this is equal to g of x.

01:53.439 --> 01:55.454
Well, the way I can take
this right hand side

01:55.454 --> 01:58.682
and get g of x is by
taking the antiderivative,

01:58.682 --> 02:00.256
taking the indefinite integral.

02:00.256 --> 02:02.559
And so what we can do, we can take

02:02.559 --> 02:06.059
the indefinite integral of both sides, dx.

02:07.375 --> 02:08.208
So dx.

02:09.267 --> 02:13.582
On the right-hand side
I'm gonna get g of x.

02:13.582 --> 02:17.221
And on the left-hand
side, I am going to get,

02:17.221 --> 02:20.383
actually let me write it this way.

02:20.383 --> 02:22.113
So on the left-hand side,
I'll just rewrite it,

02:22.113 --> 02:24.563
I'm gonna get the indefinite integral of,

02:24.563 --> 02:27.230
I'll write it as negative two dx

02:28.341 --> 02:30.091
over one minus two x.

02:31.478 --> 02:32.764
And that's just another way of writing

02:32.764 --> 02:34.585
what I have on the left-hand side here.

02:34.585 --> 02:37.531
This is equal to g of x, right?

02:37.531 --> 02:39.062
If I take the antiderivative of this

02:39.062 --> 02:40.601
or an antiderivative of this,

02:40.601 --> 02:44.629
is this g of x, where the
constant would've been zero.

02:44.629 --> 02:47.212
So I'll say is equal to g of x.

02:49.330 --> 02:51.457
And so the key here is well,
what's the indefinite integral

02:51.457 --> 02:54.927
of this stuff, and you
might immediately recognize

02:54.927 --> 02:57.430
what I have here in the bottom,

02:57.430 --> 02:59.742
I have its derivative here on the top.

02:59.742 --> 03:01.905
If I consider this to be u.

03:01.905 --> 03:04.745
If I say u is equal to one minus two x,

03:04.745 --> 03:06.346
this is just u substitution,

03:06.346 --> 03:08.952
then du is equal to the derivative of this

03:08.952 --> 03:12.641
with respect to x which
is negative two dx.

03:12.641 --> 03:15.352
And so I have the du right over here.

03:15.352 --> 03:17.096
So let me rewrite all of this.

03:17.096 --> 03:21.013
I can rewrite this as
the integral of du over u

03:23.990 --> 03:25.573
is equal to g of x,

03:27.050 --> 03:30.550
and this we can rewrite as the natural log

03:31.999 --> 03:34.749
of the absolute value of u plus c

03:35.969 --> 03:37.552
is equal to g of x.

03:38.670 --> 03:41.591
And then we can undo our u substitution,

03:41.591 --> 03:45.033
and so for u I'll undo,
I will substitute back

03:45.033 --> 03:46.722
the one minus two x.

03:46.722 --> 03:49.424
So I can write the natural log of

03:49.424 --> 03:53.591
the absolute value of one
minus two x is equal to

03:56.212 --> 03:58.369
plus c, I don't wanna for get that,

03:58.369 --> 04:00.536
plus c is equal to g of x.

04:03.275 --> 04:06.107
And so the next thing we wanna
do is well, what is our c?

04:06.107 --> 04:07.974
And the easiest way to figure out c is

04:07.974 --> 04:10.307
let's substitute zero for x.

04:11.260 --> 04:13.666
And so let's think
about this a little bit.

04:13.666 --> 04:17.565
So if I put a zero
here, if I put x equals,

04:17.565 --> 04:18.666
actually let me just write it again.

04:18.666 --> 04:21.805
So I have the natural
log, if I say x is zero,

04:21.805 --> 04:23.411
this is gonna be the natural log

04:23.411 --> 04:26.284
of the absolute value of one plus c

04:26.284 --> 04:28.496
is equal to g of zero.

04:28.496 --> 04:30.158
So what's g of zero?

04:30.158 --> 04:32.872
G of zero, everyone of these
terms is equal to zero.

04:32.872 --> 04:35.157
G of zero is equal to zero.

04:35.157 --> 04:36.931
So is equal to zero.

04:36.931 --> 04:39.879
Well, natural log of one is just zero.

04:39.879 --> 04:43.474
So zero plus c is equal to
zero, c is equal to zero.

04:43.474 --> 04:44.853
So there you have it.

04:44.853 --> 04:47.032
I just took the
antiderivative of both sides

04:47.032 --> 04:48.911
of this equation right over here.

04:48.911 --> 04:51.890
I figured out, when I
substituted zero for x,

04:51.890 --> 04:55.097
then okay, my constant
here is going to be zero,

04:55.097 --> 04:58.350
and I get that, I get that this series,

04:58.350 --> 05:00.517
this g of x is equal to

05:01.631 --> 05:05.127
the natural log of the absolute
value of one minus two x,

05:05.127 --> 05:06.961
is equal to the natural
log of the absolute value

05:06.961 --> 05:08.895
of one minus two x.

05:08.895 --> 05:11.232
Now, if we keep this restriction

05:11.232 --> 05:12.707
that we're in this open interval,

05:12.707 --> 05:14.330
then this is always going to be positive.

05:14.330 --> 05:17.183
And we wouldn't have to
write the absolute value,

05:17.183 --> 05:21.154
but we could be safe by
writing the absolute value.

05:21.154 --> 05:22.021
But there you go.

05:22.021 --> 05:24.157
Using the fact above,
we found the function

05:24.157 --> 05:25.977
that corresponds to this following series.

05:25.977 --> 05:28.238
And I guess you could
say that the trick of it

05:28.238 --> 05:30.446
was recognizing that

05:30.446 --> 05:34.113
the series up here is a derivative
of the series down here.

05:34.113 --> 05:36.775
And so this is going to be the derivative

05:36.775 --> 05:38.775
of the following series,

05:40.879 --> 05:42.838
and so we just took the
antiderivative of both sides,

05:42.838 --> 05:45.857
or the indefinite integral of both sides.
